This project aims to provide mass and balance calculation sheets for
specific aircraft. The intention is that the sheets should be usable
without an internet connection after a sheet has been downloaded to
the local computer (iPad etc.).

The publish page for the sheets is here:

http://kaltsi.github.io/Mass-and-Balance/

If you want to create an m&b sheet for your own plane, take a look at
the existing specification files under the specs subdirectory. Then
create a new file there and run the export.sh script. The script will
apply the specs to the template.html file and create the plane
specific m&b sheets.

The spec files also exist in JSON form (specs/<registration>.json), which is
what the generator reads. The JSON is generated from the .specs files with:

    python3 tools/specs_to_json.py specs/

The .specs files use upper-case names; the converter maps them to readable lower-case
keys in the JSON:

    ACFT            -> registration                 MOM_DRAW_MAX   -> envelope.moment_axis_max
    ACFT_TYPE       -> aircraft_type                MASS_DRAW_MAX  -> envelope.mass_axis_max
    DATA_ORIGIN     -> data_source                  TRANSLATE_X    -> envelope.chart_x_offset
    LITRE2KILO      -> fuel_density_kg_per_l        LND_LIMIT_X1   -> envelope.landing_limit_arm_start
    MOMENT_ARMS     -> envelope.moment_arms         LND_LIMIT_X2   -> envelope.landing_limit_arm_end
    TOF_MASS_LIMITS -> envelope.takeoff_mass_limits INVALID_DATA   -> invalid_data
    LND_MASS_LIMITS -> envelope.landing_mass_limits LOAD_POINTS    -> load_points

Load points are a list of objects with the keys: name, row_type, label (en/fi), unit,
default, min, max, show_max, moment_arm, step, save.

The keys are written in reading order rather than alphabetically: the aircraft identity,
then the chart geometry under envelope (polygon arms and mass limits, the landing limit
arms, the axis maxima, the x offset), then the load points.

Two conventions of the .specs format become explicit fields:

  - a negative max in a .specs file means "do not print the limit". In the JSON the max
    is positive and show_max is false. The template re-encodes the sign when it calls
    l_point(), because the page code tests max > 0.
  - the LP_<type>_ prefix becomes row_type: interactive, fuel_flow, checkbox, computed,
    computed_reversed.

Load point names stay upper-case: they are JavaScript variable names and the keys of the
saved values in the sheet. step is omitted when the spec has no step.

The generator is parse_template.py, which renders template.html.j2 with Jinja2:

    ./parse_template.py template.html.j2 specs/oh-kas.json out

export.sh runs it for every spec file. parse_template.sh (with template.html) is the
original shell implementation, kept for comparison. The Python version writes the same
file apart from number formatting (2.300 becomes 2.3) and quoting, which now comes from
the JSON instead of the spec text. A spec key that is not set falls back to the default
written in the template.

The template variables are the JSON keys (registration, aircraft_type, data_source,
fuel_density_kg_per_l, envelope, invalid_data) plus the load point lists: load_points
(every load point, in spec order) and extra (the load points that are not part of the
fixed calculation chains). Values are formatted for JavaScript with the js filter.

The load point name placeholders (lndw, tow, fuel, ...) resolve to the load point name
when the spec has that row, otherwise to the variable name written in the template
(g_lw, g_tow, ...).

To run it in a container:

    docker build -t mass-and-balance .
    docker run --rm -v "$PWD/out:/app/out" mass-and-balance specs/oh-kas.json out

Generated output can be checked against the reference sheets in ref2/ with:

    python3 tools/compare.py out ref2
